WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.160
Even the worst person can occasionally do a&nbsp;
good thing. In the movies, it happens with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.040
some regularity. Keep watching for some bad guys&nbsp;
who redeemed themselves. A little, at least.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.560
Few MCU characters undergo such radical&nbsp;
evolution as Thor's half-brother Loki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.800
and many fans would agree that Loki switched&nbsp;
from supervillain to superhero a while ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.520
But in 2013's "Thor: The Dark World," he's&nbsp;
still fresh from his mass murder in "Avengers."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.440
If nothing else is proof that he's still got a&nbsp;
toe in the dark side, there's his aid to Kurse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.520
during the Dark Elf assault on Asgard and his&nbsp;
subjugation of Odin at the end of the film.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.680
But we still see a more heroic side to the&nbsp;
trickster god in the Dark Elf world. When Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.320
attempts to destroy the Aether with his lightning,&nbsp;
Loki rushes to shield Jane Foster from any harm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.600
Moments later, when Kurse lobs one of&nbsp;
the black hole grenades at the heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.040
Loki nearly sacrifices himself to&nbsp;
push Jane out of the way. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.200
when Kurse proves too powerful for Thor to&nbsp;
handle alone, it's Loki's deceit that kills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.720
the Dark Elf after he secretly attaches&nbsp;
a black hole grenade to the monster.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.800
In 1983's "Return of the Jedi," Luke Skywalker&nbsp;
rejects the repeated advice of Yoda and Obi-wan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.680
who insist there is no salvation possible for&nbsp;
Darth Vader. Luke willingly surrenders himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.680
to Imperial forces, believing Anakin&nbsp;
Skywalker is still alive within Vader.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.200
Vader brings Luke to Palpatine, and&nbsp;
ultimately, Luke must once more duel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.720
his father, but this time, his victory&nbsp;
nearly pushes him to the dark side.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.960
In the end, Luke's faith in his father is&nbsp;
redeemed. As Palpatine tortures Luke with force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.880
lightning, Vader looks back and forth between his&nbsp;
son and the emperor, eventually choosing what no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.920
one expects but everyone prays for. Rather than&nbsp;
let his son die, he lifts Palpatine from the floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.320
and — battered by the emperor's lightning —&nbsp;
hurls his former master down a long shaft.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.680
In the end, Anakin not only saves&nbsp;
his son but proves he really is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.800
Chosen One the late Qui-Gon Jinn&nbsp;
knew he was so many years ago.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.120
In 2004's "Spider Man 2," Otto Octavius, aka&nbsp;
Doc Ock, proves to be a much more sympathetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.200
villain than his predecessor Green Goblin.&nbsp;
After the robotic arms he initially uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.280
only to aid him in his scientific pursuits are&nbsp;
fused to his body, we learn the arms possess a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.560
malevolent intelligence which begins influencing&nbsp;
everything Octavius does. Octavius is transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.280
into an obsessed monster who will do anything,&nbsp;
including kill, to realize his scientific goals.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.840
Thankfully, by the end of the film, Octavius&nbsp;
is able to emerge from the arms' influence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.720
redeem himself. In the climax, Octavius'&nbsp;
experimental fusion reactor is going wild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.560
and endangering not only Spider-Man&nbsp;
and Mary Jane but the entire city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.000
Finally able to assert his dominance&nbsp;
over the robot arms, Octavius uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.560
them to save New York by plunging the reactor&nbsp;
into the East River. The last we see of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.120
Octavius' seemingly lifeless body follows&nbsp;
the reactor toward the bottom of the river.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.720
"I will not die a monster."

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.000
Long before they introduced us to The Dude of "The&nbsp;
Big Lebowski," Joel and Ethan Coen gave audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.440
the 1990 gangster film "Miller's Crossing."&nbsp;
Gabriel Byrne stars as Tom Reagan, a high-ranking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.840
adviser in the Irish mob who goes rogue when his&nbsp;
boss starts getting edged out of business by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.600
Italian rival, the unpredictable Johnny Caspar.&nbsp;
Tom's risky gambit is a long con — confusing both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.680
friend and foe alike so he can win Caspar's&nbsp;
confidence and destroy him from the inside.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.080
Through sheer relentlessness, one person&nbsp;
is able to sniff out Tom's game — Caspar's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.720
brutal right-hand man Eddie Dane, aka the Dane.&nbsp;
Toward the end of the film, Tom is summoned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.600
Caspar's home, where the Dane waits to kill him.&nbsp;
At first, Caspar goes along with the Dane's plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.760
doing nothing as he chokes the life out&nbsp;
of Tom. But just as Tom begins to fade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.600
Caspar crushes the Dane's nose with a&nbsp;
fireplace shovel, saving Tom and yelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.600
that it was the Dane who double-crossed&nbsp;
him. Minutes later, Caspar says to Tom,

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.560
"Look at this, kid. Something&nbsp;
I try to teach all my boys!"

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.133
He strides over to his desk, gets a&nbsp;
gun, and shoots The Dane in the head.

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:32.434
"Always put one in the brain."

00:03:32.434 --> 00:03:34.640
For much of 2017's "Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.760
Ragnarok," Skurge isn't particularly impressive.&nbsp;
Not only is he the only warrior at the Bifrost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.680
who doesn't lift a hand to stop Hela when she&nbsp;
arrives in Asgard, but even after he becomes her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.160
so-called executioner, he pretty much just fields&nbsp;
Hela's evil monologues and lets the army of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.880
dead and Fenris do all the heavy lifting. In the&nbsp;
final battle, rather than commit to either side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.880
Skurge disguises himself and boards&nbsp;
the refugee ship, hoping to escape.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.840
However, after Hela summons one of her spikes to&nbsp;
stop the refugee ship and her undead minions begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.800
pouring into the vessel, Skurge finally finds&nbsp;
his courage. He unveils his beloved rifles — Dess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.760
and Troy — and mows down the unliving soldiers&nbsp;
before they can overtake the ship. Sacrificing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.600
his own escape for that of his brethren, Skurge&nbsp;
leaps from the ship and fights off the army of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.720
the dead as the ship pulls away. Like his comic&nbsp;
book counterpart's heroic last stand in 1985's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:27.840
"Thor" #362, Skurge ultimately dies for Asgard, as&nbsp;
Hela kills him once she sees he's switched sides.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.600
One of the most memorable scenes&nbsp;
in 1993's "Jurassic Park" is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.200
unveiling of the gargantuan T-Rex.&nbsp;
After escaping her enclosure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.600
she takes one of the first human victims of the&nbsp;
film when she devours the lawyer on the toilet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.760
She comes close to killing Hammond's grandkids&nbsp;
when she nudges their car off a ledge and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.920
injures the chaos theorist Ian Malcolm enough to&nbsp;
keep him on his back for the rest of the film.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.640
But in the end, it's the T-Rex who proves to be&nbsp;
the heroes' salvation. In the film's final act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.120
Hammond's grandchildren are hunted by the deadly&nbsp;
velociraptors in the visitor's center. Ellie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.640
Grant do their best to help the children, but&nbsp;
eventually, all four of them are trapped by two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.960
of the hungry beasts in the center's main room.&nbsp;
Just as one of the raptors is about to strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.160
the T-Rex reappears, snatches it off the floor&nbsp;
with its jaws, kills it, and begins eating it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.160
As the second raptor attacks the T-Rex,&nbsp;
the heroes take the opportunity to escape.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.800
In the 1988 action comedy "Midnight&nbsp;
Run," everybody wants to capture the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.800
fugitive accountant Jonathan Mardukas.&nbsp;
The luckless bounty hunter Jack Walsh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.400
has Mardukas in custody for most of the&nbsp;
film, but they're pursued by the FBI's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.720
Special Agent Alonzo Mosely, the mob,&nbsp;
and rival bounty hunter Marvin Dorfler.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.840
In Texas, Walsh and Mardukas are cornered by two&nbsp;
mob goons. The heroes find an unlikely savior in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.440
the form of the brutal Marvin Dorfler, who&nbsp;
knocks both goons out with the butt of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.600
shotgun. Walsh has no intention of giving his&nbsp;
bounty up to Dorfler, so he handcuffs himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.000
to Mardukas and seemingly throws away the&nbsp;
key. Dorfler loads them both into his car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.160
and they soon find themselves pursued by a&nbsp;
helicopter with a gangster sharpshooter on board.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.920
Eventually things get desperate enough that&nbsp;
Walsh convinces Dorfler to give him a gun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.120
and the former takes out the chopper&nbsp;
with a shot to the tail rotor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.280
As Dorfler celebrates their salvation, Walsh&nbsp;
sucker-punches him, knocking him unconscious.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.320
Dissimilar to the Marvel Comics, it’s rare&nbsp;
that X-Men's old enemy Magneto would ally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.000
himself with his rivals or ever join their&nbsp;
team. The first time something like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.560
happens in the film adaptations, however,&nbsp;
is in the aptly named "X2: X-Men United."

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.480
After a confrontation with the Boston police&nbsp;
turns violent outside Bobby Drake's home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:25.440
the X-Men's Blackbird is pursued by a pair of&nbsp;
fighter jets. By dialing her powers up to 11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.480
Storm is able to get both pilots to eject but&nbsp;
not before one of them fires off two missiles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.520
Jean takes out one of the missiles&nbsp;
with her telekinesis, but the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.360
detonates close enough to the Blackbird&nbsp;
to send it spinning to the ground below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.480
As the jet drops, it inexplicably slows and&nbsp;
finally comes to a halt mere feet from the ground.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.040
From outside the jet, we see Mystique and Magneto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.800
with the latter extending his arm and using&nbsp;
his powers to keep the jet from crashing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.800
As the X-Men begin to realize what's&nbsp;
happened, Magneto jokes to Mystique,

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.720
"When will these people learn how to fly?"

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.000
In the 1993 romantic crime drama "True&nbsp;
Romance," things change in a big way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.480
for comic book store clerk Clarence and former&nbsp;
sex worker Alabama. This is when a combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.360
of vigilante justice and dumb luck deposits a&nbsp;
suitcase full of uncut cocaine in their laps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.120
They manage to get wealthy movie producer&nbsp;
Lee Donowitz to take the drugs off their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.280
hands for a hefty sum, and the deal takes&nbsp;
place in an expensive LA hotel suite.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.880
Unbeknownst to anyone else in the suite,&nbsp;
Donowitz's assistant Elliot is working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.040
as an informant for the LAPD. Once the&nbsp;
deal is finalized, the cops charge in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.200
but Donowitz's private security guards&nbsp;
refuse to stand down. The standoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:33.360
gets more complicated when the mobsters who&nbsp;
want their drugs back burst into the suite.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.240
Surprisingly, the standoff turns violent because&nbsp;
of the unarmed Donowitz. The producer goes wild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.800
when he realizes Elliot betrayed him and throws a&nbsp;
tray full of hot coffee in his assistant's face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.680
Detective Nicholson opens fire in response, and&nbsp;
soon everyone is shooting. Elliot, Donowitz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.080
all of Donowitz's security, all the cops,&nbsp;
and almost all of the mobsters quickly die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.400
One of the gangsters makes&nbsp;
it as far as the hotel lobby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.240
where he's gunned down by uniformed&nbsp;
police. The only survivors are Clarence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.160
Alabama, and their friend Dick, though&nbsp;
Clarence loses an eye in the exchange.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:05.680
Who would’ve thought that a deadly three-way&nbsp;
shoutout would become their saving grace?

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.760
In 2001's "The Lord of the Rings: The&nbsp;
Fellowship of the Ring," during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:14.160
heroes' trip through Moria, Gandalf had some&nbsp;
wise words for Frodo. He tells him that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.720
should have pity on Gollum's, as Bilbo did&nbsp;
when he spared Gollum’s life years prior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.320
Gandalf believes Gollum has a part&nbsp;
to play in there story still, saying,

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.520
"The pity of Bilbo may rule the fate of many."

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.120
The words prove prophetic more than once.&nbsp;
First, in spite of Gollum's inevitable betrayal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.000
Frodo and Sam would never have&nbsp;
made it to Mordor without him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.520
Second, in "The Two Towers" it's Gollum&nbsp;
who rescues Frodo when the hobbit is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:41.360
mesmerized by the ghostly lights of&nbsp;
the Dead Marshes and nearly drowns.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.360
Finally, there's the destruction of the One&nbsp;
Ring. When he finally makes it to Mount Doom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.160
like the human Isildur before him, Frodo is&nbsp;
overcome by the ring and decides to keep it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:54.160
He puts it on, immediately drawing the attention&nbsp;
of Sauron [SOW-ron] and his wraiths. It's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.320
because of Gollum's timely intervention —&nbsp;
chomping Frodo's finger off — that Sauron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.240
is defeated. In the struggle that follows,&nbsp;
both Frodo and Gollum fall over the edge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.600
While Sam saves Frodo, Gollum descends into the&nbsp;
lava below, dying and taking the ring with him.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.840
It may have not been Gollum’s virtuous intention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.040
but his lust for the ring&nbsp;
became the salvation for many.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:18.000
His accidental sacrifice not only saved Frodo from&nbsp;
the ring’s power, but also the rest of the world.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:22.160
The replicant Roy does some&nbsp;
terrible things in the 1982&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:26.160
sci-fi classic "Blade Runner," but in the&nbsp;
film's climax he chooses to save the life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:29.760
of Deckard. Despite the fact that he’s&nbsp;
the hero who is not only hunting him but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.400
has already killed two of the three replicants&nbsp;
who worked with Roy, including his lover Pris.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:38.720
After Pris' death, Roy pursues Deckard through&nbsp;
the Bradbury Building, knowing he only has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.440
minutes to live and presumably resolving to take&nbsp;
Deckard with him. Deckard manages to escape the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:48.800
building to the rooftops but loses his gun in the&nbsp;
process. Unarmed and facing the super-strong Roy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:53.760
Deckard has no choice but to flee across the&nbsp;
rooftops, but he's no Spider-Man. His jump is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.080
short on his way from one roof to the other, and&nbsp;
he begins slipping from the opposite ledge. Rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.960
than letting him fall, Roy easily leaps to the&nbsp;
ledge, waits until Deckard finally loses his grip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:08.080
and grabs the Blade Runner's wrist. With one hand,&nbsp;
Roy lifts Deckard and deposits him to safety.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.320
In his final moments, Roy tells Deckard about&nbsp;
some of the amazing things he's seen in space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.320
regretting that those moments will be&nbsp;
lost in time, like tears in the rain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:19.920
Considering his words, it seems likely&nbsp;
Roy chooses to save his opponent not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.080
out of forgiveness or any sudden surge&nbsp;
of moral righteousness. Rather, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:29.200
as a man seconds from death, he values life —&nbsp;
even that of an enemy — more than vengeance.

